    },
    {
      "id": "alloy_system_10",
      "name": "Al-Mo-Nb-Ta-Ti-V-Zr alloy",
      "material_category": null,
      "evidence": [
        {
          "page": "6",
          "section": null,
          "quote": "While Al-Mo-Nb-Ta-Ti-V-Zr alloys suffer from B2 phase instability above 1200 °C, recent studies have identified more thermally stable B2 phases.",
          "chunk_id": "068c9ba3cdd66c99:part:002"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "alloy_system_11",
      "name": "HfMoNbTaRuWZr alloy",
      "material_category": null,
      "evidence": [
        {
          "page": "6",
          "section": null,
          "quote": "Frey et al. [24,25] reported B2-HfRu phases with thermal stability up to 1500 °C – 1600 °C in multi-principal element alloys of the HfMoNbTaRuWZr family.",
          "chunk_id": "068c9ba3cdd66c99:part:002"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "alloy_system_12",
      "name": "Ti-Al-X alloy (X = Nb, Mo, Ta)",
      "material_category": null,
      "evidence": [
        {
          "page": "6",
          "section": null,
          "quote": "In Ti-Al-X (X = Nb, Mo, Ta) based systems, ternary B2 phases are more stable than the binary Ti-Al, likely due to higher configurational entropy from additional elements.",
          "chunk_id": "068c9ba3cdd66c99:part:002"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "alloy_system_13",
      "name": "AlMoNbTaTiVZr alloy",
      "material_category": null,
      "evidence": [
        {
          "page": "6",
          "section": null,
          "quote": "However, frequent observation of AlxZry type phases (ordered omega type) in the AlMoNbTaTiVZr family suggests their relative stabilization over the multicomponent B2, especially at lower temperatures.",
          "chunk_id": "068c9ba3cdd66c99:part:002"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "alloy_system_14",
      "name": "Al0.25NbTaTiZr alloy",
      "material_category": null,
      "evidence": [
        {
          "page": "6",
          "section": null,
          "quote": "A recent study on Al0.25NbTaTiZr alloy showed a stable BCC + B2 microstructure at 1000 °C, transitioning to a three-phase BCC + B2 + AlxZry microstructure at lower temperatures [87].",
          "chunk_id": "068c9ba3cdd66c99:part:002"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "alloy_system_15",
      "name": "ferritic superalloy (Fe-Al-X)",
      "material_category": null,
      "evidence": [
        {
          "page": "6",
          "section": null,
          "quote": "Ferritic superalloys, based on Fe-Al-X systems, are optimized for applications below 1000 °C, while RSAs (including RCCAs) are designed for extreme temperatures (1300 °C – 1800 °C).",
          "chunk_id": "068c9ba3cdd66c99:part:002"
        }
      ]
    }
